<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Odisha</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Orissa</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Odisha, known as Orissa until 2011, is a state on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bay-of-bengal">Bay of Bengal</a> in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, covering the ancient region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalinga">Kalinga</a>. A separate province of Orissa was formed in 1936, and in 1948 and 1949 the many small <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/princely-state">princely states</a> of the region were merged into it. It is known for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jagannath">Jagannath</a> temple at Puri and the Sun Temple at Konark.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/odisha.jpg" alt="Odisha" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Subham9423 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Konarka_Temple.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Odisha lies in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, bordering <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jharkhand">Jharkhand</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-bengal">West Bengal</a> to the north, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chhattisgarh">Chhattisgarh</a> to the west and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andhra-pradesh">Andhra Pradesh</a> to the south, with a coastline on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bay-of-bengal">Bay of Bengal</a>. Its coastal plain, which includes the lake Chilika, is rich in silt deposited by six major rivers: the Subarnarekha, Budhabalanga, Baitarani, Brahmani, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahanadi">Mahanadi</a> and Rushikulya. The region is also known as Utkala, a name that appears in India's national anthem. The terms Odisha and Orissa derive from the ancient Prakrit Odda Visaya, found in the Tirumalai inscription of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajendra-chola-i">Rajendra Chola I</a>, dated to 1025.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Acheulian tools of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-paleolithic">Lower Paleolithic</a> point to early human settlement. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalinga">Kalinga</a> is mentioned in ancient texts such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahabharata">Mahabharata</a> and the Vayu Purana, and Baudhayana describes it as not yet influenced by Vedic traditions. Its borders coincide with those of modern Odisha. The Mauryan emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashoka">Ashoka</a> conquered Kalinga in 261 BCE, the eighth year of his reign. His own edicts record about 100,000 people killed and 150,000 captured, and he is said to have turned to pacifism and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuttack">Cuttack</a> became the capital of the region around 1135 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anantavarman-chodaganga">Anantavarman Chodaganga</a>. In the 15th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarala-das">Sarala Das</a>, who translated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahabharata">Mahabharata</a> into Odia, called the land Odisha, and the inscriptions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kapilendra-deva">Kapilendra Deva</a> of the Gajapati Kingdom (1435–67) on temple walls in Puri use the same name. The British Indian government demarcated the modern boundaries, and the Orissa Province was established on 1 April 1936 from the Odia-speaking districts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bihar">Bihar</a> and Orissa Province, the Madras Presidency and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-provinces">Central Provinces</a>. After 1948 the capital was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhubaneswar">Bhubaneswar</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Odisha is a state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> with over 41 million inhabitants. Odia, spoken by 82.70 per cent of the people at the 2011 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>, is its official language and one of the classical languages of India. The Jagannath Temple at Puri, about 200 feet high, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lingaraja-temple">Lingaraja Temple</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhubaneswar">Bhubaneswar</a> are major <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> pilgrimage sites, and part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konark-sun-temple">Konark Sun Temple</a> survives.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Odisha” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Alauddin Husain Shah (r. 1494–1519) conquered the kingdom of Kamata in 1498 and campaigned in <b>Orissa</b>, Tripura and the Brahmaputra valley, though attempts to subdue the Ahom kingdom failed.</blockquote><a href="/empire/bengal-sultanate">Bengal Sultanate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Rajendra I conquered <b>Odisha</b> and the Pala dynasty of Bengal, reached the Ganges, completed the conquest of Rajarata in Sri Lanka, and in 1025 launched naval raids on Srivijaya and the Burmese kingdom of Pegu; an inscription says he captured or plundered 14 places, identified with Palembang, Tambralinga…</blockquote><a href="/empire/chola-empire">Chola Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>…Clive's victory at the Battle of Plassey in 1757 deposed the Nawab of Bengal Siraj-ud-Daulah in favour of Mir Jafar, and the Battle of Buxar in 1764 forced the emperor Shah Alam II to appoint the Company diwan, or revenue collector, of Bengal, Bihar and <b>Orissa</b> in 1765.</blockquote><a href="/empire/company-rule-in-india">Company rule in India · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Devapala’s inscriptions claim victories over the Utkalas of <b>Orissa</b>, the Hunas, the Dravidas and the rulers of Pragjyotisha in Assam, claims that are exaggerated but suggest real campaigns to the south and east.</blockquote><a href="/empire/pala-empire">Pala Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The empire's greatest commander was Krishnadevaraya (r. 1509–1529), who defeated the Gajapati rulers of <b>Odisha</b> in campaigns between 1513 and 1519, taking Udayagiri and Kondavidu, and in 1520 defeated Ismail Adil Shah of Bijapur at the Battle of Raichur.</blockquote><a href="/empire/vijayanagara-empire">Vijayanagara Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Under the Mughals zamindars had not owned the land, only the right to collect its revenue, but in 1793 the Permanent Settlement made the zamindars of Bengal, Bihar and <b>Orissa</b> proprietors of their land in return for a fixed annual rent.</blockquote><a href="/societies/british-raj">Social Hierarchy of British India</a></li></ul></section>
